(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608124800")
  (:adr/title "Which detectors actually gate anything, and five that assert PASSED without measuring")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-12")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2608120600" "ADR-2608121000" "ADR-2608123000" "ADR-2607300900"))
  (:adr/body
  "## The question

This workspace has accumulated a great many verification scripts. **How many of
them actually gate anything?** Four more were built today, and **none of the
three ADRs recording them mentions `gates.edn` or fleet-ci at all** — they
shipped without an answer to who runs them.

## Five scripts assert a named security control and measure nothing

`scripts/security-gate-{abac-audit,audit-trail-test,georestores-test,
hsm-export-test,paging-test}.cljs`.

Measured, and re-confirmed independently: **all five contain zero
`process.exit` and zero `exitCode` assignments.** They print `PASSED` and return
0 unconditionally.

- One **computes sha256 by returning the literal string `deadbeef…` regardless
  of input.**
- One prints hardcoded page-out counts.
- One hardcodes a Keychain rejection exit code **without ever invoking
  `security`.**

And they **name themselves G-004 through G-008**, asserting that key export,
geo-restore, ABAC deny-by-default and audit trails are verified.

**These are worse than unwired detectors, because their existence reads as
coverage of named controls.** Nothing runs them today, and **wiring them would
make the theatre load-bearing** — a gate that always passes is worse than no
gate, because it occupies the slot where a real one would go.

## Four wired gates are permanently red, which is the same silence in uniform

`root-repository-roles` **0 pass / 867 fail**, `root-permit-index` 0/282,
`root-observatory-registry` 0/269, `root-itonami-org-id` 0/268.

**A standing red is indistinguishable from a new red.** 867 consecutive failures
is not a signal anyone can act on. Only `root-permit-index` has a diagnosis —
CLAUDE.md already records that its input is never in the shipped tree, so it is
structurally impossible to green. **The other three have not been diagnosed.**

## The four permanent reds, diagnosed — and the counts I quoted were misleading

**`manifest/fleet-ci.edn` is a ROLLING WINDOW, not the full history.** Its
earliest receipt is 2026-08-04; older versions in git hold receipts back to
2026-07-16. So `0 pass / 867 fail` is a statement about the current window only,
and for one of these four it materially misled me.

### `root-repository-roles` — it WAS green, and the minute it broke is known

**Last passed 2026-08-03T11:05:26Z. First failed one tick later**, a single
clean pass-to-fail transition. So this gate **has been demonstrated to work in
both directions in production** — stronger evidence than the break/unbreak note
it lacks. It has been red for roughly **1,010** consecutive runs, not 867.

**The verifier is fail-fast**, so a receipt only ever shows the FIRST violation
and the true count has never been visible. Measured per-repo: **4 violations of
23**, all one class — a child repo's declared `:depends-on` drifting behind its
own `deps.edn`. The other 19 pass, so every later assertion in that verifier has
simply never been reached since 2026-08-03.

**The structural problem underneath is worse than the violations.** This gate is
attached to `root` but **is not a function of root's tree** — it fetches HEAD
for 23 repos over the network. **Root cannot make it green, and the receipt is
stamped with root's sha while measuring something else**, so re-running the same
root sha on a different day gives a different verdict. It measures HEAD, not the
pin, so **it is not attributable to any commit.**

**CLOSED — green for the first time since 2026-08-03, and one of the four was
the dependency rather than the declaration.**

`provider` declared nothing wrong: `io-ipld` was in `:deps`, but **zero `src`
namespaces reference it** — it appears in exactly one test, as an *independent
oracle* checking our own canonical wire format. It is not shipped. **It was
moved to the `:test` alias rather than added to the declaration**, beside
another dependency already test-only for the same reason. Making the check agree
with a mistake would have been the easier fix and the wrong one.

The other three were stale declarations, each dependency confirmed as a real
`src` require. One had drift **in both directions**: a commit swapped one
dependency for another and never touched the declaration.

**One judgement call, and the way it was handled is the point.** `dag-cbor` sits
in `:deps` while no `src` namespace references cbor — it is a deliberate co-pin
at the same sha another dependency already resolves, so two layers agree on the
codec byte for byte instead of inheriting a transitive pin. **Deleting it would
also have made the gate green, and would have quietly handed that choice away.**
It was declared instead, with the reasoning in the commit — **reviewable rather
than buried.**

**The later assertions ran for the first time since 2026-08-03, and they pass.**
Role direction, acyclicity and prefix declarations all executed and none failed;
the three new edges are permitted without an exception. One non-failing note
remains: a repo declaring an authority library while shipping no git deps has
that claim **reported as unverified rather than as passed.**

**Greenness here is still not a property of any commit.** The same root sha will
give a different verdict tomorrow if any of those 23 repos moves — including
going red again with no root commit involved. And it measures HEAD, **not the
west pin**, so it says nothing about the tree this superproject actually builds
against.

### `root-observatory-registry` — five of nine are the gate being stale

A 2026-08-11 rename moved repo names to the role plane and added a `:repo` key,
**keeping `:name` as the ledger identity** — the registry documents the contract.
The gate checks `:name` and has never heard of `:repo`. **It was right when
written; the thing it guards changed under it.**

One is a genuine registry gap. One flags **a deliberately documented
requirement** — routing a summary outside the checkout is mandatory, and the
invariant cannot express *this path must NOT be in the repo*. And two are
genuine by the letter while **the harm they claim is falsified by measurement**:
`plutil -lint` accepts all ten plists and the job the gate says cannot load **is
loaded**.

It has **never once been green** across its entire lifetime, and unlike its
neighbours it carries **no break/unbreak record** — so it has never been shown
to discriminate at all.

**Resolved 2026-08-12. It is green, and it has now been shown to discriminate.**

All four classes were fixed in the order above, and none of the nine was fixed
by deleting an invariant — the `:change-rate` / `:importance` / `:blocked-by` /
`:unmeasured` discipline that only this gate checks is intact and is exercised
by four of the sixteen demonstration cases.

- **Invariant 5** now resolves the repo as `(or (:repo o) (:name o))`, which is
  the contract the registry itself documents (`:repo` omitted means `:name` is
  the repo name). Five violations vanish.
- **The genuine gap** was `com-etzhayyim-rasen`, the one entry the rename left
  without a `:repo`; its west project is `actor-rasen`, under the org the entry
  already named.
- **Invariant 4 was conflating two things.** What it exists to catch is a
  *relative* path, which silently means a different place depending on where the
  process started; an absolute path does not have that defect. It now rejects
  relative paths as before, and requires an absolute path outside the repo to
  carry `:args-outside-repo` with a reason — so the deliberate case is expressible
  and an accidental write outside the repo is still caught. hayari declares it,
  citing the concrete reason: the default target is tracked, so every run would
  dirty the west checkout and `west update` would then skip the project.
- **Invariant 11 was true, and its stated reason was false.** Measured:
  `plutil -lint` returns OK for all ten plists, and the job the gate said could
  not load was loaded with a PID from a file byte-identical to the flagged repo
  copy. But a *conforming* XML parser is a different matter — expat rejects both
  flagged files at exactly the offending lines and accepts the other eight. XML
  1.0 section 2.5 forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so the files really
  are not well-formed; the harm is not that launchd ignores them but that no XML
  tool other than Apple's deliberately lenient parser can read them. **The check
  was kept, its rationale corrected, and the two comments fixed** — all ten now
  satisfy both parsers.

**The demonstration CLAUDE.md requires now exists**, in
`scripts/fleet-ci/gates/observatory-registry-discriminate.cljs`: sixteen cases,
each breaking one invariant in a throwaway copy, asserting a non-zero exit *and*
that the violation reported is that invariant's own message — so a red caused by
some other invariant cannot be mistaken for detection — then restoring and
asserting exit 0. Baseline green, 16/16, harness exits 0. The record lives in
the gate's own header so that editing an invariant surfaces it in the diff.

**Writing the harness immediately paid for itself**: two cases first reported
*broken but still green*, and both were faults in the breaking, not the gate —
one replaced an occurrence of the key inside a comment and left the real key
intact, the other renamed a tag to a string that still contained the substring
being searched for. Reviewed by eye, both would have counted as demonstrations.
**A check on a check is theatre by the same rule as the check itself.**

### `root-itonami-org-id` — it is correctly reporting an undeployed fix

Six of eight domain pairs disagree with the rule. One root cause: the endpoint
re-exports a **checked-in compiled artifact**, and its source **was fixed on
2026-08-10, two hours after the gate's first red** — with a docstring naming the
bug — **and never rebuilt.** The gate has been right for two days.

A caveat for whoever rebuilds: for two domains the rule's reversed form does not
match the existing org names, so a correct intake will mint ids that disagree
with two live orgs. That is an owner decision, not a gate bug.

### `root-permit-index` — the recorded diagnosis holds, and is not the only cause

Run **on a machine that does have the input**, it still fails: the committed
projection is genuinely behind its sources. **Re-homing it alone would look like
a completed fix and then stay red.**

### What generalises

**None of the four is a broken check in the `npx` sense** — no laptop-versus-node
difference affected any verdict. Two of the four are attached to `root` while
measuring something root does not contain, which is the same structural error as
`root-permit-index` in a form that is easy to miss, because those two **do**
produce a verdict rather than failing to run. A gate that answers about someone
else's HEAD is not a gate on this repo; it is a monitor wearing a gate's badge.

## Most of today's detectors cannot be root gates, and that is structural

CLAUDE.md's `root-permit-index` finding generalises: **a fleet gate ships only
the target repo's own tree**, so anything reading `orgs/` can never be green
there. That covers `verify-vendored-copies`, `verify-namespace-collisions`,
`verify-duplicate-registrations`, `verify-require-graph`, `annex-custody-verify`,
`west-orphan-audit`, `dataset-monotonic-check` and roughly a dozen migration
audits. Two of them say so in their own headers.

**So the answer for these is not `add a gates.edn line` — it is that they need a
different home.** A local scheduled tick on a machine that has `orgs/`, which is
what the uninstalled plists below were.

Reading outside the repo entirely: `audit-gftd-scripts` inspects `$HOME/.gftd`,
and **a fleet node's home is a different machine's**, so a node result would be
meaningless. `annex-custody-verify` needs B2 credentials, which invariant 3
forbids on nodes.

**Gatable as-is, root-tree only**: `verify-adr-identity`, `closing-check`,
`cross-repo-change-guard`, `verify-no-babashka`, the bmc test runner, and two
fleet-ci unit tests that are pure logic with no I/O.

One useful clarification about the constraint: *self-contained in one file*
binds the **wrapper**, not the detector. The working gates here are thin
wrappers that `execSync` the repo's own multi-file script out of the extracted
tree, each dying loudly if the target is missing after extraction.

## The cheapest recovery in the survey is an uninstalled plist

Five launchd plists live in `scripts/` and **are not installed**. One of them,
`com.gftd.tsukuru-maturity-tick`, is **the only caller of three verifiers**, and
one of those guards something that has already been lost — ADR-2607252000
records SHIRO and PICO ep01 assets **actually lost**, B2 unreachable and IPFS
pins garbage-collected, **irreversibly**. The other two cover customer names and
part numbers leaking through annexed **file paths** even where content is
encrypted, and consent-tier datasets being silently merged.

**Nothing was wrong with the check. The scheduler was never loaded.**

## `root-rename-residue` is gated, and its required proof is missing

It is the one detector from today that is wired, and it is well built — the
wrapper refuses to report success unless the output carries an `N cases OK`
summary with N at or above the fixture count, **specifically so a git-less
environment running only the pure cases cannot go green.** 20 receipts, 0
failures, and it is live in production inside `cleanup-land.cljs`.

CLAUDE.md's requirement — **break the target, see exit 1; unbreak it, see
exit 0** — was recorded nowhere. The 21 fixture cases include negatives, which
is evidence the *classifier* discriminates; **it is not evidence the *gate* goes
red.**

**Done 2026-08-12. The record lives in the wrapper's own header comment**
(`scripts/fleet-ci/gates/rename-residue-check.cljs`), as a table of break ->
exit code -> message, because that puts it next to the floors it exercises: a
diff that lowers `min-cases` or deletes a floor now carries the line naming that
floor's exit code. Summary of what was measured, each by actually breaking a
copy of the tree and running the gate in the fleet's own `<dir>`-first shape:

- collapsing `classify` to a single verdict — either direction — **exits 1**;
- deleting the git-fixture half **exits 94**, and the underlying test in that
  run **exited 0 printing `PASS — 13 cases OK`** — the floor is what stopped it,
  not the test;
- deleting the summary `println` **exits 93**;
- removing any of the three required paths **exits 90**, each naming which;
- putting a flag before `<dir>` **exits 90** — the argv hazard fails closed here
  rather than producing a false green.

The floors are distinguishable from one another. Two further findings: the gate
is **green on a tree filtered to `.cljs` only** (350 files at `3b0371f4`,
`:min-files 300` — 50 of margin), so unlike `root-permit-index` it is not
structurally unable to pass on a shipped tree, though that margin is thin and
moves: it read 355 an hour earlier, before a sibling session retired the five
`security-gate-*` scripts; and **a forged summary line still passes** — a test
entry reduced to `(println \"PASS — 21 cases OK, 0 failures\")` reports green,
since `N` is a floor and not a cross-check. That is outside what these floors
claim: they close *environment* failures, and an edit to the test body is closed
by diff review, not by the gate.

**Limit of the demonstration**: it ran on this laptop, where npm 11.12.1
misparses `npx --yes nbb --classpath X file.cljs` (measured: the argument is
resolved as `github:scripts/…`, `npm ERR! code 128`), so a PATH shim calling
`nbb` directly stood in for `npx`. It establishes that the gate's *decision
logic* goes red and green as claimed; it does **not** establish that the npx
invocation path works on a node, nor the converse. Node-side evidence is the
receipt.

## One correction to the brief that produced this survey

I told the surveying agent that `audit-gftd-scripts` reports 3 live
**shallow-fetch** violations. As run at survey time it reports 3 violations of
kind **`:shell-script`**, and no shallow-fetch violations at all.

**Both readings are correct at their own moment**: a sibling agent fixed the
three shallow-fetch violations earlier the same day, taking the count from 6 to
3 and leaving exactly the shell-script ones. The surveying agent measured after
that fix and reported the discrepancy rather than reconciling it to my claim,
which is the right behaviour — **it could not have known, and asserting my
number would have been worse than contradicting it.**

## Ranked by what each would have caught, and when

1. **`verify-vendored-copies`** — weeks of live drift on payment-confirmation
   paths, one copy missing the guard whose absence accepts an unconfirmed
   payment as confirmed. Needs a home with `orgs/`.
2. **`verify-require-graph`** — a deleted namespace broke 12 repos for 17 days.
   Already paid once.
3. **Install `com.gftd.tsukuru-maturity-tick`** — recovers three verifiers at
   once, one guarding custody that has already cost irreversible asset loss.
   Cheapest recovery here.
4. ~~`verify-adr-identity`~~ — **GATED, merge `ccb47c1fa06`, and it was red.**

   Two `:adr/id` collisions introduced 2026-08-08 had gone unnoticed for four
   days, plus two ambiguous references pointing at one of them. **A prior session
   had OBSERVED them and recorded them verbatim in its own close, and declined
   to fix.** Fixed here by the rule *the later arrival moves*, which
   independently agreed with where every existing citation points. 25 collisions
   down to the 23 in the debt table, whose docstring says the table must never
   grow — so *add it to the table* was never an available fix.

   **A correction to my brief.** I said dropping `90-docs/adr` would make the
   script report zero ADRs and exit 0. **It does not** — tested with the floor
   disabled: it still exits 1, because a fourth check fires and all 23 debt-table
   entries stop colliding. **The detector cannot pass vacuously on an empty
   corpus.** The `:min-files` floor is still right to exist — it turns an obscure
   23-line stale-debt spew into one legible line — but it is defence in depth,
   not the only thing standing between the fleet and a vacuous pass.

   **The two layers catch different mistakes**, which is why both are needed:
   filtering to `.cljs` drops the ADRs and the floor stops it; filtering to
   `.edn` drops **the detector itself**, which the floor passes and the gate
   guard catches at exit 90. **That second row is not hypothetical** — the
   fleet's own tick carries a comment recording it happening on 2026-07-29.

   **Proven on real fleet hardware**, not this laptop: shipped to a node over
   ssh and run through the production invocation shape. Fixed tree exit 0;
   duplicate id injected exit 1; removed again exit 0; corpus missing exit 90;
   detector missing exit 90. Locally it first hit the documented npm breakage —
   **and the third floor caught that too**, refusing to report a pass for a run
   that may not have scanned anything.

   Limits stated: one node of ten, `tick.cljs` not run end to end (so signing,
   status posting and scheduling are unexercised), and the `:min-files`
   rejection shown arithmetically and by reading the code rather than by
   executing the tarball filter.
5. **`audit-gftd-scripts`** — belongs in a SessionStart hook, because it is the
   only thing that looks outside the repo at all.
6. `verify-namespace-collisions`, `verify-duplicate-registrations` — real,
   dated, ungatable in root.
7. ~~Prove `root-rename-residue` can go red.~~ **Done 2026-08-12**; the record
   is in the wrapper header, and the one hole it does not close (a forged
   summary line) is named there.
8. **Diagnose the four permanent reds**, then fix, re-home or retire each. A red
   that never changes is as silent as no detector.
9. **Delete or rewrite the five `security-gate-*` scripts.**

## What could not be determined

Whether the three undiagnosed permanent reds fail for the `root-permit-index`
reason or for a genuine violation — that needs reading each failure detail.
Whether `root-rename-residue` was manually demonstrated to fail before landing;
**absence of a record is not proof it was not done** — moot as of 2026-08-12,
since it has now been demonstrated and the demonstration left a trace. ## DETERMINED: silently dropped, and the documentation still says they run

The five uninstalled plists were **not retired** — they were dropped, and this
workspace's real retirement mechanism proves the difference. When
`com.gftd.hayari-tick` was genuinely retired its plist was moved to an
`_archive/` directory **and an ADR recorded the move**. Ten older plists sit in a
dated launchd archive. **None of the five is in either.**

Three ADRs affirmatively record three of them as installed, bootstrapped and
kickstart-verified — and one **demonstrably worked**, repairing a seven-document
backlog on 2026-08-08, one commit per document, exactly as designed. **No ADR
records removing any of them and no logs survive for any of them.**

**The larger finding is the documentation gap.** Six skills state that a resident
loop calls them every cycle. **None is loaded**, and three of the six **have no
plist anywhere** — not in the repo, not on the machine. The surviving plists also
cite sibling jobs that do not exist.

**So the documentation asserts a schedule that has not existed for days**, which
is the same shape as this document's other findings, one layer up: a detector
nobody runs reads as coverage; **a loop nobody runs reads as a routine.**

**A caution against reinstalling them blind.** Three of the five launch an
autonomous model loop with permission checks skipped, on 8-hour, 1-hour and
6-hour intervals, one of them fanning out to two dozen parallel agents per wave,
and they commit and push. **That is a materially different decision from
installing a read-only monitor** and needs explicit consent. Exactly one of the
five — a detection-only sync probe whose own header states it never writes to
`main` — is the safe one.

## What could not be determined

Whether the three undiagnosed permanent reds fail for the `root-permit-index`
reason or for a genuine violation — that needs reading each failure detail.
Whether `root-rename-residue` was manually demonstrated to fail before landing;
**absence of a record is not proof it was not done** — moot as of 2026-08-12,
since it has now been demonstrated and the demonstration left a trace.")))
